## The idea

Build it in layers. **Filter**: drop anything below the minimum severity — the cheapest check, done first. **Write** to every sink, each in its own try/catch so one broken sink cannot stop the others. **Throttle**: remember, for each identical log (same severity, function, line and message), when its window started and how many copies were suppressed. **Summarise**: when the window has expired and the same log comes again, first write one summary line stamped with the window's first time, then the new line. **Purge** windows that expired with nothing suppressed, so the table cannot grow forever. **AsyncLogger**: callers drop the line into a bounded queue and return at once; one background thread writes to the sinks. When the queue is full, the line is **dropped and counted** — logging must never block a request.

## Step by step

1. Format every line as "{now} {severity} {func}:{line} {message}".
2. Log: if sev < min → return. Build the key (sev, func, line, msg). Under the lock: if a window exists and now < start + window → suppressed++ and return. If it expired and suppressed > 0 → prepare a summary line. Reset the window (start = now, suppressed = 0), or create one if there is none.
3. Outside the lock: write the summary (if any), then the line. Writing outside the lock means a slow sink does not make every caller wait in line.
4. PurgeExpired: under the lock, collect keys whose window has expired and suppressed == 0, remove them, return how many.
5. AsyncLogger: a BlockingCollection<string> with a bounded capacity. Log uses TryAdd (never blocks): true → accepted++, false → dropped++.
6. A background thread reads with GetConsumingEnumerable, writes to every sink (counting sink failures), and counts written. DisposeAsync calls CompleteAdding and waits for the thread to finish draining.

## Worked example

**Input:** window 1000 ms; Warn f:1 "disk" logged at 0, 10, 20, then 1500

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| t=0 | no window → create {start 0, suppressed 0}; write "0 Warn f:1 disk" |
| t=10 | 10 < 0 + 1000 → suppressed = 1, nothing written |
| t=20 | suppressed = 2, nothing written |
| t=1500 | window expired, suppressed 2 → write "0 Warn f:1 disk [suppressed 2 identical in 1000ms]" |
| t=1500 (same call) | reset window {start 1500, suppressed 0}; write "1500 Warn f:1 disk" |
| t=3000 | expired, suppressed 0 → no summary, just the line |

**Result:** 4 lines in total: the first, the summary, 1500, 3000

## Cost

Log is O(1): one dictionary lookup under a short lock. PurgeExpired is O(table size). AsyncLogger.Log is O(1) and never waits; memory is bounded by the queue capacity. The throttle table is the one thing that grows with distinct messages — that is what PurgeExpired is for.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
using System.Collections.Concurrent;

// Stages 1–4: filter, throttle identical logs, summarise what was suppressed, purge old windows.
public sealed class Logger {
    // One throttle window per identical log.
    private class Window {
        public long Start;          // when this window began (also the "first occurrence" time)
        public int Suppressed;      // copies swallowed inside the window
    }

    private readonly object _lock = new object();
    private readonly Severity _min;
    private readonly IClock _clock;
    private readonly long _windowMs;          // 0 = no throttling
    private readonly ILogSink[] _sinks;
    // "Identical" = same severity, function, line and message.
    private readonly Dictionary<(Severity, string, int, string), Window> _windows =
        new Dictionary<(Severity, string, int, string), Window>();

    public Logger(Severity min, IClock clock, params ILogSink[] sinks) : this(min, clock, 0, sinks) {
    }

    public Logger(Severity min, IClock clock, long windowMs, params ILogSink[] sinks) {
        if (windowMs < 0) throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(windowMs));
        if (sinks == null || sinks.Length == 0) throw new ArgumentException("need at least one sink");
        _min = min;
        _clock = clock;
        _windowMs = windowMs;
        _sinks = sinks;
    }

    public void Log(Severity sev, string func, int line, string msg) {
        if (sev < _min) return;                          // stage 1: cheapest check first

        long now = _clock.NowMs;
        string summary = null;

        if (_windowMs > 0) {
            var key = (sev, func, line, msg);
            lock (_lock) {
                Window w;
                if (_windows.TryGetValue(key, out w)) {
                    if (now < w.Start + _windowMs) {      // stage 2: still inside the window
                        w.Suppressed++;
                        return;
                    }
                    if (w.Suppressed > 0) {               // stage 3: say what we swallowed
                        summary = w.Start + " " + sev + " " + func + ":" + line + " " + msg +
                                  " [suppressed " + w.Suppressed + " identical in " + _windowMs + "ms]";
                    }
                    w.Start = now;                        // a new window starts with this log
                    w.Suppressed = 0;
                } else {
                    w = new Window();
                    w.Start = now;
                    _windows[key] = w;
                }
            }
        }

        // Write outside the lock, so a slow sink does not hold up other threads.
        if (summary != null) Write(summary);
        Write(now + " " + sev + " " + func + ":" + line + " " + msg);
    }

    // Stage 4: forget windows that are over and had nothing suppressed.
    public int PurgeExpired() {
        long now = _clock.NowMs;
        lock (_lock) {
            var dead = new List<(Severity, string, int, string)>();
            foreach (var pair in _windows) {
                bool expired = now >= pair.Value.Start + _windowMs;
                if (expired && pair.Value.Suppressed == 0) dead.Add(pair.Key);
            }
            foreach (var key in dead) _windows.Remove(key);   // cannot remove while looping over it
            return dead.Count;
        }
    }

    private void Write(string text) {
        foreach (ILogSink sink in _sinks) {
            try {
                sink.Write(text);
            } catch (Exception) {
                // A broken sink must not stop the other sinks or crash the caller.
            }
        }
    }
}

// Stage 5: callers never wait for I/O. Lines go into a bounded queue; one thread writes them.
public sealed class AsyncLogger : IAsyncDisposable {
    private readonly Severity _min;
    private readonly ILogSink[] _sinks;
    private readonly BlockingCollection<string> _queue;
    private readonly Thread _writer;
    private long _accepted = 0;
    private long _dropped = 0;
    private long _written = 0;
    private long _sinkFailures = 0;

    public AsyncLogger(Severity min, int capacity, params ILogSink[] sinks) {
        _min = min;
        _sinks = sinks;
        _queue = new BlockingCollection<string>(capacity);   // bounded: memory cannot grow forever
        _writer = new Thread(WriterLoop);
        _writer.IsBackground = true;
        _writer.Start();
    }

    // Returns true if the line was accepted, false if filtered or dropped.
    public bool Log(Severity sev, string msg) {
        if (sev < _min) return false;
        bool added;
        try {
            added = _queue.TryAdd(sev + " " + msg);          // does not wait: false when full
        } catch (InvalidOperationException) {
            added = false;                                    // logger is shutting down
        }
        if (added) {
            Interlocked.Increment(ref _accepted);
            return true;
        }
        Interlocked.Increment(ref _dropped);                  // full: drop it and count it
        return false;
    }

    private void WriterLoop() {
        // Ends when CompleteAdding was called AND the queue is empty.
        foreach (string line in _queue.GetConsumingEnumerable()) {
            foreach (ILogSink sink in _sinks) {
                try {
                    sink.Write(line);
                } catch (Exception) {
                    Interlocked.Increment(ref _sinkFailures);
                }
            }
            Interlocked.Increment(ref _written);
        }
    }

    public (long Accepted, long Dropped, long Written, long SinkFailures) Metrics {
        get {
            return (Interlocked.Read(ref _accepted), Interlocked.Read(ref _dropped),
                    Interlocked.Read(ref _written), Interlocked.Read(ref _sinkFailures));
        }
    }

    // Stop accepting, let the writer drain what was accepted, but never wait forever.
    public ValueTask DisposeAsync() {
        _queue.CompleteAdding();
        _writer.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Null or empty message? Unknown severity? Window of 0?**

Validate or log as-is — decide; window 0 disables throttling

**Q: Cost per Log call?**

O(1) expected; filter before the lock

**Q: Where is the bottleneck?**

Sink I/O — it must not be under the lock

**Q: 10,000 threads logging simultaneously — what is shared?**

Throttle table → short lock; the sink → off-thread

**Q: Lock or ConcurrentDictionary for the throttle table?**

Read-decide-update per key: one lock (or AddOrUpdate with care)

**Q: Logs overwhelm downstream.**

Bounded buffer, drop-and-count, sampling, batching

**Q: Millions of distinct messages.**

Purge expired windows; cap the table

**Q: What do you alert on?**

Drops and sink failures

**Q: The log sink is down for ten minutes.**

Buffer then drop with counts; fallback sink

